issue.body:
**Describe the bug**
Consider the following function

```kotlin
interface Factory

fun doSomething(factories: Set<@JvmSuppressWildcards Factory>) {
}
 // or
fun doSomething(factories: @JvmSuppressWildcards Set<Factory>) {
}
```
And later in the processor
```kotlin
// Where functionDeclaration is the doSomething function decleration
functionDeclaration.parameters.map(KSValueParameter::toParameterSpec)
 
fun KSValueParameter.toParameterSpec(): ParameterSpec {
    return ParameterSpec.builder(
        name = name?.asString().orEmpty(),
        type = type.toTypeName(),
    ).build()
}
```
Produced ParameterSpec will lose the annotations applied to the original function. 

**Expected behavior**
I would expect the KSTypeReference.toTypeName to preserve annotations applied to function parameters and TypeArguments if any.

**Describe the bug**
Since 1.17.0 ([#1833](https://github.com/square/kotlinpoet/issues/1833), "write varargs in place instead of making them an array"), KSAnnotation.toAnnotationSpec() emits vararg annotation arguments as positional values appended at the end of the argument list. When the vararg parameter is not the last parameter of the annotation and any other argument is present, the generated code interleaves named arguments with a trailing positional one, which the Kotlin compiler rejects:

```
error: Mixing named and positional arguments is not allowed unless the order of the arguments matches the order of the parameters.
```

[#1833](https://github.com/square/kotlinpoet/issues/1833) fixed the case where the vararg is effectively the last/only argument (@OptIn), but the "in place" emission assumes the vararg is last. It isn't always — Java annotations conventionally declare value() (often an array) first, e.g. SpotBugs' @SuppressFBWarnings(String[] value, String justification, ...).

Environment
KotlinPoet: reproduced on 2.2.0 and 2.3.0 (latest); 2.3.0 changelog contains no fix for non-last varargs
kotlinpoet-ksp interop
KSP: 2.3.x
Kotlin: 2.2.x

**To Reproduce**
Minimal annotation with a vararg parameter that is not last:

```kotlin
package test

annotation class Fbw(vararg val value: String, val justification: String)

@Fbw("HARD_CODE_PASSWORD", justification = "not a password")
class Subject
```

Run a trivial processor over Subject and call toAnnotationSpec() on its annotation (full runnable processor below).

Actual output (does not compile)
```kotlin
@test.Fbw(
  justification = "not a password",
  "HARD_CODE_PASSWORD",
)
```

→ Mixing named and positional arguments is not allowed ...

**Expected behavior**
Expected output (compiles)
```kotlin
@test.Fbw(
  `value` = arrayOf("HARD_CODE_PASSWORD"),
  justification = "not a password",
)
```
i.e. when the vararg parameter is not the last parameter, fall back to emitting it as a named name = arrayOf(...) argument (the pre-1.17.0 form), which is order-independent and always legal.

Real-world trigger (Java annotation)
The common real case is a Java annotation whose array value() comes first:

```Java
public @interface Fbw {
    String[] value();
    String justification();
}
@Fbw(value = {"HARD_CODE_PASSWORD"}, justification = "not a password")
public class Subject {}
```

KSP reports value as a vararg parameter, so toAnnotationSpec() produces the same broken output.

Runnable repro (KSP processor)
```kotlin
import com.google.devtools.ksp.processing.Resolver
import com.google.devtools.ksp.processing.SymbolProcessor
import com.google.devtools.ksp.processing.SymbolProcessorEnvironment
import com.google.devtools.ksp.processing.SymbolProcessorProvider
import com.google.devtools.ksp.symbol.KSAnnotated
import com.google.devtools.ksp.symbol.KSClassDeclaration
import com.squareup.kotlinpoet.ksp.toAnnotationSpec

class ReproProcessorProvider : SymbolProcessorProvider {
  override fun create(environment: SymbolProcessorEnvironment): SymbolProcessor =
    object : SymbolProcessor {
      override fun process(resolver: Resolver): List<KSAnnotated> {
        resolver.getAllFiles()
          .flatMap { it.declarations }
          .filterIsInstance<KSClassDeclaration>()
          .filter { it.simpleName.asString() == "Subject" }
          .forEach { decl ->
            decl.annotations.forEach { ann ->
              // Prints the broken positional form on 2.2.0 / 2.3.0:
              //   @test.Fbw(justification = "not a password", "HARD_CODE_PASSWORD")
              environment.logger.warn("toAnnotationSpec => ${ann.toAnnotationSpec()}")
            }
          }
        return emptyList()
      }
    }
}
```

Register it via `META-INF/services/com.google.devtools.ksp.processing.SymbolProcessorProvider `(or symbolProcessorProviders in kotlin-compile-testing / kctfork), compile the Subject source above, and observe the logged annotation. Feeding that string back into the compiler produces the "mixing named and positional arguments" error.

Root cause

`KSAnnotation.toAnnotationSpec()` collects vararg values into varargValues and appends them positionally after all named members. That is correct only when the vararg parameter is the last parameter. The fix is to emit the vararg as a named arrayOf(...) member whenever it is not last (or, more simply, whenever any named argument would precede it).
